#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72c96e3f-e296-52c6-9c78-1b926625fe29" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "e2f973d4-5d1e-5e35-84d3-277d1069d6f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12873" :question/problem "Calculate $10\\div 5^0 + (3^2-5).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10÷{5}^{0}+(9-5)=10÷{5}^{0}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $5^0=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10÷{5}^{0}+4 & = \\\\ 10÷1+4 & = \\\\ 10+4 & = \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97e3c93e-ceff-5bdb-905f-0c245aa0d362" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Negative Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "6f8c20ab-5dcb-5cb2-bf0f-8bc14e019e00" :question/math-academy-id "e-12874" :question/problem "What is the value of  $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} ?$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}\\times (-2)^{-2}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 4}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}\\times 1}{3\\times \\cancel{4}}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128872 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "222a5d13-74e9-5f1b-81d5-f38a6b80870c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Both Zero and Negative Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "c6b6a911-843f-5c96-a792-ebd807b5d60e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12875" :question/problem "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n\\left(-4\\right)^{-2}  = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{(-4)}^{-2}÷{(1.5+\\frac{2}{15})}^{0} & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16}÷1 & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{32}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{33}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72c96e3f-e296-52c6-9c78-1b926625fe29" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5b659f-28dd-59a3-9a57-f1486c9e4227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480761} {:answer/id #uuid "29cfea49-9f6f-5f9a-bae2-ad1521302d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480814} {:answer/id #uuid "37d0909f-7fbe-5218-bffa-a0ad06486fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480847} {:answer/id #uuid "7bab4a86-358b-505d-abc3-83284ddcbca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480969} {:answer/id #uuid "fee2a83e-1972-5806-bfaf-ca4442d6dce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d0909f-7fbe-5218-bffa-a0ad06486fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480847} :answer-field/id #uuid "fedc1fab-9a1c-5cb6-864b-93ad4589f535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6932ab11-a677-5ac7-a52f-b39238ad7b0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-100221" :question/problem "${(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${0.03}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {6}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {6}^{2}÷3-3 & = \\\\ 36÷3-3 & = \\\\ 12-3 & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97e3c93e-ceff-5bdb-905f-0c245aa0d362" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Negative Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031fe889-8bcf-55b6-9e2c-5bc76ff27045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480742} {:answer/id #uuid "18283892-3eb2-55cd-b35f-59a9010c6395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480785} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9c3feb-96eb-537a-af4e-a8b745ab09c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480824} {:answer/id #uuid "43f5d7d0-17b2-565d-a98f-1e82335af6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480869} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d07389-9d7e-558b-836a-ab159ef21592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43f5d7d0-17b2-565d-a98f-1e82335af6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480869} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed2c6d7-4afd-5487-8b46-d9d9acb8c258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "372dbe69-5a97-5673-bcbc-f7d304887030" :question/math-academy-id "q-100293" :question/problem "${7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6+1}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{7}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 7}{7\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times \\cancel{7}}{3\\times \\cancel{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "222a5d13-74e9-5f1b-81d5-f38a6b80870c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Both Zero and Negative Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480828} {:answer/id #uuid "76d69649-f0c6-5771-848d-71954873581b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480957} {:answer/id #uuid "b381b4b1-c1bc-52ab-8b7e-21ef5860d874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481079} {:answer/id #uuid "e6602306-1f53-57b5-91c7-8b39e35659a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481178} {:answer/id #uuid "fdeff659-436b-5f34-ac81-a6409e40fce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480828} :answer-field/id #uuid "93d546fe-e93e-5d3c-8f24-4f640019211e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e5e796d-b724-597d-a614-e8af03095fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-100301" :question/problem "Calculate ${(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, notice that\n$\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}$\nis not zero. Any non-zero number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}={(\\frac{2}{1})}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3} & = \\\\ 1-11\\times 8 & = \\\\ 1-88 & = \\\\ -87\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72c96e3f-e296-52c6-9c78-1b926625fe29" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213a2f49-0016-5875-84c9-889f8190ab98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "213a2f49-0016-5875-84c9-889f8190ab98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480805} :answer-field/id #uuid "392e6cec-195a-576a-925d-034fd1114be2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfd0e220-31b0-52ce-8e2c-55afd941a790" :question/math-academy-id "q-175947" :question/problem "$15+(-3)^{3}÷{7}^{0}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15+(-3)^{3}÷{7}^{0} & = \\\\ 15+(-27)÷1 & = \\\\ 15+(-27) & = \\\\ 15-27 & = \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97e3c93e-ceff-5bdb-905f-0c245aa0d362" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Negative Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff108b6d-b341-5dec-bcea-441b760861b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff108b6d-b341-5dec-bcea-441b760861b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481234} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bea4dc1-0a50-536e-8358-41147aeb0b3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6433a3cf-883a-54aa-9f03-bdc753346e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-175958" :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+(5+1)^{-2}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+(5+1)^{-2}=\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+{6}^{-2}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${6}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{36}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+{6}^{-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{36}\\times \\frac{2}{1}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 2}{36\\times 1}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{36}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{10+1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72c96e3f-e296-52c6-9c78-1b926625fe29" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e30d5f8-b029-5e8f-994f-f9d19469b0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e30d5f8-b029-5e8f-994f-f9d19469b0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480946} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2d042f-d653-58ff-8c3e-46fccccb3798" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00632209-b439-5ff0-a973-a59862d161d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-178582" :question/problem "${8}^{0}+{2}^{3}\\times 4=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${8}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{0}+{2}^{3}\\times 4 & = \\\\ 1+8\\times 4 & = \\\\ 1+(8\\times 4) & = \\\\ 1+32 & = \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72c96e3f-e296-52c6-9c78-1b926625fe29" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417d82f2-955e-5809-8bcc-e9b8832280b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417d82f2-955e-5809-8bcc-e9b8832280b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480864} :answer-field/id #uuid "609c007e-c457-5fc9-a429-2f89995ec208" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9362880e-cb69-5b9a-a326-3f6c64c489b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-178596" :question/problem "$((-4)^{0}-5)÷{2}^{2}+7=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nNotice that $(-4)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((-4)^{0}-5)÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (1-5)÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ -4÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ -4÷4+7 & = \\\\ -1+7 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "222a5d13-74e9-5f1b-81d5-f38a6b80870c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Both Zero and Negative Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481235} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a275f8-e12f-531b-9ed6-2c3eda31cf0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dbc86c1-eb39-5436-87d8-eb16a5d4d270" :question/math-academy-id "q-179401" :question/problem "$(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that ${2}^{-4}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{16}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1+16}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{17}{16}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
